      <section aria-labelledby="recon">
        <h2 id="recon">
          Making Sinks Bulletproof: Version Guards &amp; Watermarks
        </h2>
        <p>
          Even with perfect partitioning, delivery is at-least-once: retries,
          consumer restarts and replays deliver the same event again, and a
          rewind, a repartition or a re-snapshot can show your sink an older
          change after a newer one. Correctness comes from an idempotent sink
          keyed on the primary key and ordered by the source's log position,
          so your target table needs a primary key and a log-position column:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong><code>id</code>:</strong> The primary key of the entity.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong><code>source_lsn</code> (the log position):</strong> The
            position of the change in the source's transaction log, copied
            from the event as-is. It increases in commit order for a given
            key within one source log (row locks serialise writers per key),
            so it says which change is newer. Pick the field that is unique
            per change:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <strong>PostgreSQL:</strong> <code>source.lsn</code>, where the
                connector provides a per-change position (positions are
                unique in practice, but verify this for your connector
                version).
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>SQL Server:</strong> compare
                (<code>commit_lsn</code>, <code>change_lsn</code>,
                <code>event_serial_no</code>) as a tuple.
                <code>commit_lsn</code> alone is shared by every change in a
                transaction.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Oracle:</strong> <code>scn</code>, with
                <code>rs_id</code> and <code>ssn</code> to separate changes
                that share an SCN (verify the ordering semantics for your
                Debezium version). <code>commit_scn</code> is shared by the
                whole transaction, and <code>scn</code> can repeat across
                changes in it.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>MySQL:</strong> binlog file, <code>pos</code> and the
                row index, because several rows can share one binlog row
                event.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          <strong>Uniqueness.</strong> The guard below assumes the position is
          unique per change for a key, or is paired with a sequence (as the
          BigQuery example does with <code>seq</code>). If an INSERT and an
          UPDATE of one key in one transaction share a position, a strict
          <code>&lt;</code> skips the UPDATE and the row stays stale. An equal
          position is always skipped; that is correct only when it is the same
          change.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Comparing.</strong> Compare positions numerically or as a
          typed tuple, never as free text: the text LSNs
          <code>0/9</code> and <code>0/10</code> sort the wrong way round,
          SQL Server LSNs and Oracle SCNs arrive as strings in Debezium, and
          MySQL needs a (file, pos[, row]) comparison. Fixed-width hex strings
          (SQL Server) compare correctly as text only if all values are the
          same width.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Lineage.</strong> Positions are comparable only within one
          source log lineage. After a failover that loses the old
          primary's tail, a point-in-time restore, a move to a different server
          or cluster, or a binlog reset (not a normal rotation or a new
          replication slot on the same cluster), positions can restart lower,
          and the guard skips every change until the new position passes the
          stored one. Version the lineage, for example a
          <code>source_epoch</code> column that you compare first, then the
          position. A GTID is not listed above because GTIDs are comparable
          only within one server UUID, and file plus position is likewise not
          portable across servers. A primary-key change is a delete plus an
          insert, so &ldquo;for a given key&rdquo; does not span it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Not a Kafka offset.</strong> A Kafka offset is a position in
          the Kafka log, not the source log. A connector restart re-emits
          earlier changes at new, higher offsets, so an offset guard would
          accept them as newer. Use offsets for consumer progress only, never
          as the row version.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Not a timestamp.</strong> Do <em>not</em> use
          <code>ts_ms</code> as the version. Clocks on different hosts
          disagree (clock skew), and several changes to one row can share the
          same millisecond, so the comparison ties. The envelope
          <code>ts_ms</code> (when the connector processed the event) is later
          on every replay, and snapshot rows carry the snapshot time, so a
          stale value can win. <code>source.ts_ms</code> keeps the original
          commit time on a replayed streaming event, but it is still
          millisecond-coarse and clock-dependent. Keep <code>ts_ms</code> for
          lag metrics and, at most, as a secondary tie-break.
        </p>
        <p>
          The consumer then uses a <code>MERGE</code> (or
          <code>INSERT...ON CONFLICT</code>) statement with a crucial
          <code>WHERE</code> clause:
          <strong
            >only update the target row if the incoming event's log position is
            greater than the position already stored.</strong
          >
          A replay of the same change carries the same position it had the
          first time, so the strict comparison skips it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Deletes.</strong> A physical <code>DELETE</code> removes the
          version column along with the row, so a late, replayed older update
          would match nothing, hit <code>WHEN NOT MATCHED</code> and resurrect
          the row. Retain a delete marker instead: a <code>deleted</code> flag
          plus the delete's position on the row, or a separate table of
          deleted keys and their positions. The cost is that delete markers
          need a retention and cleanup policy, and reads must filter
          <code>deleted = false</code>. This is the &ldquo;remove or
          flag&rdquo; choice described on
          <a href="{{ '/materialization/' | url }}">Materialization</a>. Ignore
          Kafka null-value tombstone records here: they follow a delete event
          for log compaction and carry no position.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Watermark (aggregates only):</strong> event time is the right
          axis for closing time windows, but never for deciding which version
          of a row wins. If events can arrive late, advance a per-table
          watermark (“safe up to T-Δ”) before finalizing aggregates. Late
          events ≤ Δ update rows; older ones are quarantined or appended to a
          corrections table.
        </p>
        <div class="codebox">
          <h3>Production-Ready Idempotent Sink Pattern (Postgres)</h3>
          <pre><code>-- Assumes target table has a primary key `id`, a `deleted` flag and a column
-- `source_lsn` (BIGINT) holding the log position carried in the event, e.g.
-- Debezium `source.lsn`. It is a log position, not a clock time.
-- Assumes the connector provides a per-change position (unique in practice
-- for Postgres). If several changes to one `id` can share a position, add a
-- `seq` tie-break as in the BigQuery example below.
-- MERGE applies as a single atomic statement, but it is NOT immune to
-- concurrency: under READ COMMITTED a racing INSERT of the same `id` can
-- still raise a unique_violation. For high-contention upserts prefer
-- INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE, or retry the MERGE on conflict.

MERGE INTO target_table T
USING (
  SELECT
    :entity_id::bigint AS id,      -- use your key's type
    :payload::jsonb AS payload,    -- NULL for a delete: column must be nullable
    :op::text AS op,
    :source_lsn::bigint AS lsn
) AS S
ON (T.id = S.id)
WHEN MATCHED AND T.source_lsn < S.lsn THEN
  -- Only update if the incoming event is later in the log;
  -- a replay of the same change (same lsn) matches nothing.
  -- A delete is kept as a delete marker (deleted = true + its lsn), so a
  -- late older update cannot resurrect the row. Reads filter deleted = false.
  UPDATE SET
    payload = S.payload,
    deleted = (S.op = 'd'),
    source_lsn = S.lsn
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
  -- If the record doesn't exist, create it (a delete creates a delete marker)
  INSERT (id, payload, deleted, source_lsn)
  VALUES (S.id, S.payload, (S.op = 'd'), S.lsn);</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="codebox">
          <h3>Tie-break within one log position (sequence) — BigQuery example</h3>
          <pre><code>-- `lsn` is the source log position. `seq` orders events that share one
-- position, e.g. the event's index within its transaction (Debezium
-- `transaction.total_order`, where the connector provides one; it needs
-- transaction metadata enabled).
-- Not shown: the delete marker (see Deletes above). `@lsn` is INT64, so SQL
-- Server / Oracle string positions need a typed comparison first.
MERGE `dw.target` T
USING (SELECT @id AS id, @lsn AS lsn, @seq AS seq, @payload AS p) S
ON T.id = S.id
WHEN MATCHED AND (T.lsn &lt; S.lsn OR (T.lsn = S.lsn AND T.seq &lt; S.seq)) THEN
  UPDATE SET payload = S.p, lsn = S.lsn, seq = S.seq
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
  INSERT (id, lsn, seq, payload) VALUES (S.id, S.lsn, S.seq, S.p);</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="muted">
          A primary-key upsert with a log-position guard makes the sink
          idempotent for updates: transport stays at-least-once, and duplicates
          and late arrivals become no-ops (deletes need the retained delete
          marker described above). That is exactly-once
          <em>processing</em>.
          End-to-end exactly-once across independent systems is not achievable;
          see
          <a href="{{ '/exactly-once/' | url }}">Exactly-Once Semantics</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="audit">
        <h2 id="audit">Audit loops (detect &amp; repair)</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Windowed row-counts:</strong> compare source vs sink counts
            over event-time windows (for example 5-minute buckets).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Windowed checksums:</strong> compute hash aggregates (<code
              >SHA1</code
            >
            of concatenated key fields) per window and diff.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Automatic rewind:</strong> on mismatch, rewind the consumer
            to an offset before the affected window and re-process. Kafka
            rewinds per partition, so unaffected keys are re-read too; the
            idempotent, log-position-guarded sink makes that safe.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Quarantine queue:</strong> route irreconcilable records to a
            DLQ with enough context to replay or manually fix.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

